<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Kumharsain State</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Kumharsain</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Rajput hill state north of Shimla, one of the Simla Hill States restored under British protection after the Gurkha wars.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tradition holds that the ruling family descended from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> named Kirti Singh who came from the plains around 1000 and settled in the hills north of the Sutlej-Giri watershed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kumharsain was a small mountain state of terraced farms and forest, ruled by a Rana and at times a tributary of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bashahr-state">Bashahr State</a>. Little is recorded of its internal history before the 19th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gurkha</a> forces of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal</a> occupied it in the early 1800s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-nepalese-war">Anglo-Nepalese War</a> the British issued a sanad in 1815 restoring the Rana as one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simla-hill-states">Simla Hill States</a>. Kumharsain merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a> in April 1948 and is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a> district.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="princely state, 1000 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">princely state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Indian princely states</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:79.60%;width:18.87%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kumharsain</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Western Pahari</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kumharsain</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1474&amp;focus=kumharsain-state" aria-label="Kumharsain State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6681 1037 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kumharsain State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7001 1293L7364 1601M7001 1293L7364 1602M7001 1293L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7001 1293h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7001 1293h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kumharsain. <a href="/atlas?year=1474&amp;focus=kumharsain-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
